# Grandma Ruth 16 · Christmas tips (Vinicius, 2/11/2026): do this, avoid that, Christmas dinner
# 8 Christmas Dinner Mistakes EVERYONE Makes (Fix Them Before the 25th)
# Grandma Ruth: born 1944 in Mansfield, Ohio; mother Dorothy, father Walter, brother Danny, Grandma Pearl.

sc("""Sweetheart, I have cooked Christmas dinner for sixty-one years. The first time I was nineteen, newly married, and I burned the rolls, forgot the gravy, and served the turkey raw in the middle. I cried in the pantry while my husband told everybody it was delicious. So believe me, every mistake I'm about to tell you, I either made myself or watched somebody I love make it.""",
   "Christmas dinner family table turkey", "SIXTY-ONE YEARS")
sc("""Tonight I'm giving you the eight Christmas dinner mistakes I see every single year, and for every one, exactly what to do instead. My mother's way, and Grandma Pearl's way. Most of it is just planning. Get a pencil, honey. By the end of this you'll have a Christmas dinner that comes out hot, on time, and without anybody crying in the pantry.""",
   "vintage kitchen holiday cooking", "EIGHT MISTAKES")

ch("1. No plan on paper")
sc("""Mistake number one. Keeping the whole dinner in your head. You think you remember everything, and then at four o'clock you realize the sweet potatoes need the oven at the same time as the turkey, and the rolls haven't risen, and nobody bought cream for the pie.""",
   "busy kitchen cooking stress", "IN YOUR HEAD")
sc("""Do this instead. Write a timeline on paper, backwards from the minute you sit down. If dinner is at three, the turkey comes out at two. It goes in at nine. Potatoes on at one-thirty. Rolls in when the turkey comes out. My mother taped her timeline inside the cupboard door every Christmas, in pencil, and crossed off each line with a big X. I still have one from nineteen fifty-eight.""",
   "handwritten recipe list kitchen", "TAPE IT TO THE CUPBOARD")
sc("""And make the shopping list from the recipes, not from memory. Read every recipe the week before, every line, and write down every single thing, even salt and butter. Butter runs out at Christmas, honey. Every year somebody on Elm Street knocked on our door on Christmas morning to borrow a stick of butter from my mother.""",
   "grocery shopping list pencil", "EVEN THE BUTTER")

sc("""And here's the thing nobody tells young cooks. Set the table the night before. Plates, glasses, napkins, the good silver, the gravy boat with a spoon in it, every serving bowl out on the counter with a little slip of paper inside saying what goes in it. My mother did it after we went to bed on Christmas Eve. On Christmas Day there's no hunting for the big platter while the turkey gets cold.""",
   "Christmas table set night before", "A SLIP OF PAPER IN EVERY BOWL")

ch("2. A frozen turkey on Christmas Eve")
sc("""Mistake number two. Taking the turkey out of the freezer on Christmas Eve. On Christmas morning it's still a block of ice in the middle, and people start doing dangerous things, like leaving it in the sink all day or putting it in hot water.""",
   "frozen turkey thawing refrigerator", "A BLOCK OF ICE")
sc("""Do this instead. Thaw it in the refrigerator, in a pan, and give it one day for every four or five pounds. A twenty-pound bird needs four or five days, so it goes in the icebox on the Saturday before Christmas. If you're late, put it breast down in a sink of cold water and change the water every thirty minutes. Never warm water, sweetheart. Never on the counter.""",
   "raw turkey roasting pan kitchen", "ONE DAY PER FOUR POUNDS")

sc("""And avoid this, honey. Never put stuffing inside a bird the night before. Mix the dry and wet parts separately the night before if you like, but stuff the turkey right before it goes in the oven, and check the middle of the stuffing gets hot all the way through, a hundred and sixty-five degrees. Or do what I do now, and bake it in its own dish. It gets that crispy top everybody fights over.""",
   "stuffing baking dish crispy", "THE CRISPY TOP")
sc("""How do you know the turkey is done? Not by the little plastic button, sweetheart. Buy a meat thermometer. Put it in the thickest part of the thigh without touching the bone. A hundred and sixty-five degrees and it's done. My mother tested by wiggling the leg, and she was usually right, but I've had a thermometer since nineteen seventy and I haven't served a raw turkey since that first terrible Christmas.""",
   "meat thermometer roast turkey", "NOT THE PLASTIC BUTTON")

ch("3. Cooking everything that day")
sc("""Mistake number three. Trying to cook every single dish on Christmas Day. One oven, one cook, eight dishes, and a house full of people. That's how the rolls burn and the cook misses the whole party.""",
   "mother cooking holiday kitchen oven", "ONE OVEN, EIGHT DISHES")
sc("""Do this instead. Make everything you can ahead. Pies on the twenty-third. Cranberry sauce on the twenty-second, because it's better after two days. Cut the onions and celery for the stuffing the night before and keep them in a covered bowl. Make the mashed potatoes in the morning and keep them warm in a covered pot set in a pan of hot water. Grandma Pearl said the best Christmas cook is the one who's sitting down when the doorbell rings.""",
   "homemade pies cooling kitchen", "SITTING DOWN WHEN THE BELL RINGS")
sc("""Here's my mother's cranberry sauce, honey, it couldn't be easier. One bag of fresh cranberries, one cup of sugar, one cup of water. Boil the sugar and water, add the berries, and cook about ten minutes until they pop. Add the grated peel of one orange at the end. Pour it in a bowl and put it in the icebox. It sets all by itself. Nobody ever went back to the can after tasting it.""",
   "homemade cranberry sauce bowl", "UNTIL THEY POP")

sc("""And use your refrigerator like a second kitchen. The week before, clean it out completely. Throw away the old jars, wipe the shelves, and make room. You'll need it for the turkey, the cranberry sauce, the pies, the cream and the drinks. My mother called it the Christmas cleaning, and she did it every year on the eighteenth, while we kids ate everything she found in the back.""",
   "clean refrigerator shelves food", "THE CHRISTMAS CLEANING")

ch("4. Carving right away")
sc("""Mistake number four. Carving the turkey the minute it comes out of the oven. Everybody's hungry, everybody's watching, so you cut right in, and all the juice runs out onto the board. And the meat on the plate is dry before anybody even says grace.""",
   "roast turkey carving board", "ALL THE JUICE RUNS OUT")
sc("""Do this instead. Let it rest. Cover it loosely with foil and leave it alone for thirty minutes. It stays hot, I promise, and the juice goes back into the meat. That half hour is when you make the gravy, warm the rolls and get the sides on the table. My father always carved, and my mother never let him near the bird until she said so. She'd slap his hand with a wooden spoon.""",
   "roast turkey resting foil", "THIRTY MINUTES")

sc("""Here's how to carve so it looks beautiful, honey. Don't saw slices off the whole bird at the table. Take the bird to the kitchen. Cut off each leg and each breast half, whole. Then slice the breast across the grain, about as thick as a pencil, and lay the slices on a warm platter with the legs at the ends. Put a little parsley around it. It looks like a magazine, and it stays warm and juicy.""",
   "sliced turkey platter parsley", "AS THICK AS A PENCIL")

ch("5. Lumpy gravy")
sc("""Mistake number five. Dumping flour straight into hot drippings and hoping for the best. You get lumps the size of marbles, and then somebody pours it through a strainer, and there's half as much as you need.""",
   "gravy boat Christmas dinner", "LUMPS LIKE MARBLES")
sc("""Do this instead. Grandma Pearl's way. Put a quarter cup of flour in a jar with a cup of cold water, put the lid on, and shake it hard, like you're mad at it. Then pour it slowly into the hot drippings while you stir, and keep stirring until it bubbles and thickens. Salt and pepper at the end. Cold water and a jar, honey. In sixty years I've never had a lump.""",
   "glass jar flour water kitchen", "SHAKE IT LIKE YOU'RE MAD")

sc("""And never throw away the drippings, sweetheart. If there's too much fat, pour everything into a measuring cup and let it sit a minute. The fat rises to the top. Spoon it off and keep the brown juice underneath. That brown juice is the whole flavor of your gravy. The fat my mother saved in a can by the stove, for frying potatoes the rest of the week. Nothing wasted.""",
   "pan drippings measuring cup kitchen", "THE BROWN JUICE")

ch("6. Too many new recipes")
sc("""Mistake number six. Trying four new recipes from a magazine on Christmas Day. Christmas is not the day to experiment. If something goes wrong, there's no time to fix it, and the family wanted the old dishes anyway.""",
   "magazine recipes cooking kitchen", "NOT THE DAY TO EXPERIMENT")
sc("""Do this instead. One new dish, at most, and try it once in early December first. Everything else should be the dishes the family has eaten for years. That's what Christmas tastes like. My grandchildren would riot if I didn't make Pearl's sweet potatoes with the little marshmallows on top. Nobody remembers the fancy new salad. They remember what tastes like home.""",
   "sweet potato casserole marshmallows", "WHAT TASTES LIKE HOME")

sc("""Here's Pearl's sweet potatoes, so you can make them too. Boil four big sweet potatoes in their skins until soft, peel them and mash them with half a stick of butter, a third of a cup of brown sugar, a pinch of salt and a little orange juice. Spread them in a buttered dish, cover the top with little marshmallows, and bake at three fifty until the marshmallows turn golden. Watch them, honey. They burn in a blink.""",
   "sweet potatoes mashed baking dish", "THEY BURN IN A BLINK")

ch("7. Doing it all alone")
sc("""Mistake number seven. Being the only one in the kitchen. The cook works for two days and everybody else watches football. Then she's too tired to enjoy any of it, and too proud to say so.""",
   "woman alone kitchen washing dishes", "TOO PROUD TO SAY SO")
sc("""Do this instead. Give everybody a job, written down. Danny and I peeled potatoes from the time we were six. My father set the table and filled the water glasses. Grandma Pearl made the gravy. When guests ask what they can bring, say a pie, or rolls, or a bag of ice, and mean it. And the rule in my house is simple: the cook doesn't do the dishes. Whoever didn't cook, washes. That rule has saved more marriages than any preacher.""",
   "family cooking together kitchen holiday", "THE COOK DOESN'T WASH")

sc("""And don't forget the cook, sweetheart. Make yourself a plate before the platter goes around, and set it aside, or you'll end up with a wing and cold potatoes. And sit down with everybody for the prayer. My mother always stood in the kitchen doorway during grace, and one year my father went and got her chair and put it right next to his. She sat in it every Christmas after that.""",
   "family saying grace Christmas dinner", "SIT DOWN FOR GRACE")

ch("8. Wasting the leftovers")
sc("""Mistake number eight. Throwing out half the turkey on the twenty-seventh. In nineteen fifty-one, nobody I knew wasted a single bite of a Christmas turkey. A bird was too expensive. It fed us for a week.""",
   "leftover turkey refrigerator containers", "A WHOLE WEEK")
sc("""Do this instead. Within two hours of dinner, take the meat off the bones and put it in shallow covered dishes in the icebox. Eat it within four days, or freeze it. Then put the carcass in your biggest pot with an onion, two carrots and some celery, cover it with water and simmer it all afternoon. That's your soup for the cold week after Christmas. My mother called it the best meal of the whole holiday, and she was right.""",
   "homemade turkey soup pot stove", "THE BEST MEAL OF THE HOLIDAY")

ch("Grandma's rule")
sc("""So here's the whole lesson, honey. Plan it on paper. Thaw the bird early. Cook ahead. Let the turkey rest. Shake the gravy in a jar. One new dish at most. Give everybody a job. And make soup from the bones. Do those eight things and you'll be the calmest person in your own kitchen.""",
   "Christmas dinner table family candles", "CALM IN YOUR OWN KITCHEN")
sc("""And tell me in the comments, sweetheart. What's the one dish your family would riot without at Christmas? I read every one. Tomorrow I'll tell you how to wrap presents for almost nothing, so pretty nobody wants to open them. Good night, honey. Merry Christmas.""",
   "Christmas table warm light family", "WHAT'S YOUR DISH?")

for s in S:  # documentary look: four photos per scene and the six rotating camera moves
    s["cuts"], s["moves"] = 4, True

